1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
A giant 747 destroys an apartment building.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Hollywood Boulevard ruptures and sinks into a subway tunnel.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
An oil tanker explodes, killing nine.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
A tidal wave of molasses drowns 21 people.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And a freeway ramp collapses, burying workers in concrete.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On October 4, 1992, an El Al Boeing 747 freighter took off from Schiphol Airport near Amsterdam.

8
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,700
It was 5.20 p.m.

9
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:58,720
The three-man crew and one passenger were headed to Tel Aviv to deliver a routine cargo shipment.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,040
But the flight turned out to be anything but routine.

11
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:08,880
Shortly after takeoff, the co-pilot sent a distress call to the control tower.

12
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:11,620
Mayday, mayday, we have an emergency.

13
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:15,420
LL-1862, do you wish to return to Schiphol?

14
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:18,100
Roger, we have fire on engine number 3.

15
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:19,780
Roger, 1862.

16
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,560
Schiphol, LL-1862, we have an emergency.

17
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:27,960
Number 3 and number 4 engine in operative. Request to 7 for landing.

18
00:01:27,961 --> 00:01:34,740
Only seven miles into the flight, the pilot turned around and attempted to return to the airport.

19
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:42,740
His closest runway was the crosswind runway, which was, I believe he had almost 30 knots of wind directly crosswind.

20
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:51,220
And to try to line up the airplane to the runway with two engines gone on one side, limited controllability, but almost impossible.

21
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:54,800
The jet was making its final approach to the runway

22
00:01:54,801 --> 00:01:58,260
when the pilot suddenly lost control of the giant 747.

23
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:05,520
The plane plummeted at full speed toward a residential section of Belmameer near Amsterdam.

24
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,240
It was like you just was flying straight into the ground.

25
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:13,740
And he hit right at the apex of the apartment building.

26
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,420
The 747 exploded upon impact,

27
00:02:17,421 --> 00:02:20,760
and the 11-story apartment building burst into flames.

28
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:30,440
Nobody on board survived the crash, and 47 people on the ground were instantly killed in their homes as they were going about their daily lives.

29
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:35,294
The rest of the building was essentially considered unlivable.

30
00:02:35,295 --> 00:02:36,960
It was destroyed so badly.

31
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:42,640
The authorities were concerned they might have a structural collapse, so they evacuated all the tenants.

32
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,560
A team from the Netherlands led the accident investigation.

33
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,800
They invited a team from Israel and the United States National Transportation Safety Board to join them.

34
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:59,660
This was the second mysterious 747 crash in less than a year.

35
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:06,420
Ten months earlier, a China Airlines jet also lost two engines and went down in Taiwan.

36
00:03:06,421 --> 00:03:11,820
When the LL crash occurred, there were so many similarities to both cases

37
00:03:11,821 --> 00:03:15,000
that it ended up speeding up the investigations of both of them.

38
00:03:15,001 --> 00:03:20,000
The NTSB was determined to get to the root of the problem during the El Al investigation.

39
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:28,000
You take eyewitness reports, you take air traffic control tapes, you take the recorders.

40
00:03:28,001 --> 00:03:32,000
We can examine them in detail either in the field or in the lab.

41
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:39,000
And by process of elimination, you can focus down the area in which it's significant.

42
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:46,000
The primary suspects in this case were the engines, which had lost power during the flight.

43
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:48,222
A Boeing 747 has four engines.

44
00:03:48,223 --> 00:03:54,000
If you're facing forward, the number one and two engines are on the left wing,

45
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:57,150
and number three and number four engines are on the right side.

46
00:03:57,151 --> 00:04:00,000
So going left to right, there's one, two, three and four.

47
00:04:00,001 --> 00:04:05,420
747 engines are mounted to the underside of the wing.

48
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:09,180
They are attached to an engine strut with engine mounts,

49
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:12,180
and the strut is attached to the wing with fuse pins.

50
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:20,040
To prevent wing damage, fuse pins are designed to break away cleanly from the wing of the aircraft

51
00:04:20,041 --> 00:04:22,640
in the event of a sudden catastrophic engine failure.

52
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:27,780
There were several, I would have saw pieces coming off the airplane as it flew over the water

53
00:04:27,781 --> 00:04:30,340
and as it was circling around to try and get to the airport.

54
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,540
While scouring the bottom of Lake Isolmare,

55
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:36,740
investigators found all of engine number four

56
00:04:36,741 --> 00:04:38,720
and part of engine number three.

57
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:43,360
Finding the engine in the water was a significant piece of evidence

58
00:04:43,361 --> 00:04:46,360
because it showed us the strut mounting, how it had broken.

59
00:04:46,980 --> 00:04:48,500
And of course, this was kind of the key.

60
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,640
As they inspected the engine mountings,

61
00:04:51,641 --> 00:04:55,600
they began to suspect that the tragedy had been caused by a single fuse pin

62
00:04:55,601 --> 00:04:57,580
that supported engine number three.

63
00:04:57,780 --> 00:05:01,220
We found one fuse pin that are fatigue fractured.

64
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:06,720
It can start from a small pit from corrosion or a scratch.

65
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:11,400
And fatigue is kind of like if you want to break a piece of metal,

66
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:14,940
you bend it back and forth and back and forth and finally it breaks.

67
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,520
That's a fatigue fracture.

68
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:20,000
And this will happen even in the strongest airplane material.

69
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:27,000
Over the years, moisture in the air had caused a tiny corrosion pit of rust

70
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:28,120
to form in the pin.

71
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:32,160
It was just a matter of time before the fuse pin cracked

72
00:05:32,161 --> 00:05:34,000
and then broke apart completely.

73
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:37,280
When one lets go in flight like this,

74
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:41,200
it allowed the engine to rotate,

75
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,660
and of course that then overloaded the other fuses,

76
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:45,920
and that caused a separation of number three.

77
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,020
As it came loose, it came down the leading edge of the wing

78
00:05:50,021 --> 00:05:53,260
and was tearing up pieces of the leading edge as it went along.

79
00:05:53,261 --> 00:05:55,600
And as it reached where number four is,

80
00:05:55,601 --> 00:05:57,420
it was out in front of number four,

81
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,980
and essentially the airplane flew into the number three engine.

82
00:06:01,540 --> 00:06:05,600
That contact caused the damage or enough load

83
00:06:05,601 --> 00:06:07,040
to tear off the number four engine.

84
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:10,020
Losing power in two engines

85
00:06:10,021 --> 00:06:12,600
does not necessarily spell doom for an aircraft.

86
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,020
Airplanes are certified by the Federal Aviation Administration

87
00:06:16,021 --> 00:06:18,020
to fly 50% of your engines.

88
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:19,300
In this case, you have four engines,

89
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:21,420
so you should be able to fly with two of the engines.

90
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:23,080
But unfortunately, in this case,

91
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:25,360
you have a situation where both of the engines

92
00:06:25,361 --> 00:06:28,500
were all, as we mentioned, were the number three, number four engine,

93
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:30,260
and they were on the same side of the airplane.

94
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:32,760
If you put a lot of power on one side of the airplane,

95
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,180
it tends to want to roll the airplane.

96
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:35,980
And if you can't stop the roll,

97
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:40,380
we thought maybe this was one of the reasons why you lost control.

98
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,080
The added damage to the wing in the El Al incident

99
00:06:44,081 --> 00:06:46,600
contributed significantly to the controllability problem

100
00:06:46,601 --> 00:06:48,600
as opposed to just two engines and operative.

101
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:52,880
A few weeks before the accident,

102
00:06:52,881 --> 00:06:53,581
L.A.L.

103
00:06:53,582 --> 00:06:58,680
had inspected the fuse pins on the Boeing 747 for corrosion and minor fractures.

104
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:05,240
The tests used to examine the fuse pins were unable to detect the fatigue fracture.

105
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:16,560
Since the crash of the L.A.L. 747, Boeing has improved its testing procedures.

106
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:22,400
By bouncing ultrasound waves off fuse pins or passing eddy currents over them,

107
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:26,360
engineers can detect even the smallest corrosion and fatigue fractures.

108
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:32,340
Fuse pin materials have also been upgraded to prevent corrosion caused by old age.

109
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:36,700
The other fuse pins were made out of what are called carbon steel, high-strength carbon steel.

110
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:41,280
But we designed one out of stainless steel, which will take care of the corrosion problems.

111
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:45,160
And the design was such that the machining would be a lot simpler

112
00:07:45,161 --> 00:07:51,100
and would eliminate possibilities of having machine-type defects during the manufacturing process.

113
00:07:51,101 --> 00:07:54,800
and so this is subsequently what came of the accident.

114
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:58,720
It's shocking to think that so much death and destruction

115
00:07:58,721 --> 00:08:00,860
could begin with a little bit of rust.

116
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:10,080
Fortunately, since the El Al disaster in 1992,

117
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,520
there have been no other fatalities caused by engines

118
00:08:13,521 --> 00:08:15,920
unexpectedly dropping out of the sky.

119
00:08:15,921 --> 00:08:26,560
Before the LL-747 crashed, the jet airliner had accumulated 45,746 flight hours.

120
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:31,420
This is equivalent to flying non-stop for more than five years.

121
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:36,240
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

122
00:08:36,241 --> 00:08:47,300
Los Angeles has always been notorious for smog and traffic jams.

123
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:53,680
As air pollution and traffic congestion reached nightmarish proportions in the 1980s,

124
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:55,960
the city was desperate for a solution.

125
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:02,020
And the Los Angeles subway system was born.

126
00:09:02,021 --> 00:09:11,560
The underground mass transit system called the Metro Red Line was part of a $4.5 billion

127
00:09:11,561 --> 00:09:18,220
public transportation project that included a 17-mile dual-tunnel subway system to connect

128
00:09:18,221 --> 00:09:20,100
downtown Los Angeles to North Hollywood.

129
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:29,960
Three major tunneling contractors had formed the SKK venture in 1992 to dig out and construct

130
00:09:29,961 --> 00:09:34,520
the twin tunnels of the Hollywood Boulevard and Vermont Avenue legs of the underground system.

131
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:45,083
Excavation continued well into the 1990s.

132
00:09:45,084 --> 00:09:50,520
But on June 22, 1995, an infamous and spectacular accident

133
00:09:50,521 --> 00:09:55,420
made its way into the Hollywood spotlight and spread doubt about the safety of future

134
00:09:55,421 --> 00:09:57,324
tunneling under Los Angeles.

135
00:09:57,325 --> 00:10:01,200
At 11 a.m., a depression in the middle of famed Hollywood

136
00:10:01,201 --> 00:10:06,300
Boulevard suddenly ruptured and disappeared into one of the subway tunnels below.

137
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:13,340
The shocking incident occurred as construction workers were remining an 80-foot section of

138
00:10:13,341 --> 00:10:17,015
the south tunnel in the Hollywood Boulevard leg of the subway system.

139
00:10:17,016 --> 00:10:18,400
A gas line, a sewage line,

140
00:10:18,401 --> 00:10:23,037
and a high-pressure water main burst and flooded the chasm.

141
00:10:23,038 --> 00:10:25,080
Bystanders watched in awe,

142
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:28,160
as a huge crevasse appeared right in the middle of the street.

143
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:33,640
Joe Doyle from the California Occupational Safety and Health Administration

144
00:10:33,641 --> 00:10:35,980
was called in to investigate the situation.

145
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:40,960
The hole was growing right when we first pulled up there.

146
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:43,560
Debris was caving off, the dirt was caving off,

147
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:47,300
pavement, asphalt was peeling off of Hollywood Boulevard, dropping into the hole.

148
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,720
And at the same time, it was filling full of water, which is heavy.

149
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:54,420
The fallen debris had plugged up the tunnel.

150
00:10:55,420 --> 00:11:05,044
But as the water and sewage from the burst lines continued to rise, there was an increased risk that the water would break through the debris and gush through the

151
00:11:05,045 --> 00:11:05,745
tunnel.

152
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:16,520
Doyle immediately closed the entrance to the construction site at the Barnsdahl shaft, which was 400 feet down the road from the water-filled sinkhole.

153
00:11:19,140 --> 00:11:23,400
He then climbed down the shaft to make sure all the workers got out of the tunnel safely.

154
00:11:23,401 --> 00:11:26,420
They said, this thing's going to flush like a toilet.

155
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:28,140
We've got to get out of here.

156
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:32,940
Ten minutes later, the water flooded the tunnel below,

157
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:35,880
rushed down the hole, and then shot out into the shaft.

158
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:38,980
We were immediately up to water to our thighs.

159
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,480
We could see these timbers and even steel shooting out of there.

160
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:44,860
It just looked like Armageddon.

161
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:48,820
It was very, very fortunate that no one got killed.

162
00:11:48,821 --> 00:11:54,260
To prevent further cave-ins from devouring the rest of Hollywood Boulevard,

163
00:11:55,040 --> 00:11:57,480
SKK needed to plug up the hole fast.

164
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:01,420
They filled the hole with lean mix, as I called it.

165
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:06,920
Lean mix is sand and gravel, but mixed with very little cement,

166
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:09,320
just enough to give it some strength.

167
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:12,580
It was filled up all the way to the street surface.

168
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:16,380
When the hole was finally stabilized,

169
00:12:16,381 --> 00:12:19,720
the city of Los Angeles repaved Hollywood Boulevard.

170
00:12:21,160 --> 00:12:25,740
But it would take a year before engineers figured out how to deal with the disaster below.

171
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:33,700
The initial tunneling of the three-mile Hollywood Boulevard leg had gone smoothly.

172
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:37,200
To build the foundation for each tunnel,

173
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:42,120
workers erected a temporary support structure out of consecutive concrete rings

174
00:12:42,121 --> 00:12:44,280
that would hold up the tunnel walls.

175
00:12:44,281 --> 00:12:49,080
These critical rings would become the primary lining of the tunnels

176
00:12:49,081 --> 00:12:53,760
It would take about 8,000 of the 4 foot wide rings

177
00:12:53,761 --> 00:12:56,440
To hold up the 3 mile stretch of the twin tunnels

178
00:12:56,441 --> 00:12:57,740
In the Hollywood Red Line

179
00:12:57,741 --> 00:13:01,980
The rings were constructed in the tail of the tunneling machine

180
00:13:01,981 --> 00:13:03,780
As it plowed through the ground

181
00:13:03,781 --> 00:13:06,760
After the ring has been erected

182
00:13:06,761 --> 00:13:10,860
They put in hydraulic expansion device inside the ring

183
00:13:10,861 --> 00:13:13,000
And expand the ring outwards

184
00:13:13,001 --> 00:13:18,280
so that it gets into contact very tightly, pressing against the earth.

185
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:21,180
After that, contact has been achieved.

186
00:13:21,940 --> 00:13:25,500
The shape of the ring, which is now expanded, is being locked in place

187
00:13:25,501 --> 00:13:29,720
by putting hardwood wedges in the expanded joints.

188
00:13:30,140 --> 00:13:33,880
And the ring is just locked in place, pressing against the earth and supporting the earth.

189
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,620
The stability of the primary liners was essential to the structural integrity of the tunnels.

190
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Each closed ring had the capacity to hold up 200 tons of earth above a tunnel.

191
00:13:50,001 --> 00:13:55,000
The circle supports itself because it's being subjected to compression.

192
00:13:55,001 --> 00:14:01,000
It's sort of like an eggshell when it's complete and you press from the outside with uniform pressure around.

193
00:14:01,001 --> 00:14:08,000
An eggshell, even though it's very thin, it is very strong in compression.

194
00:14:08,001 --> 00:14:13,720
After two years of digging under Hollywood Boulevard, the initial support structure was finally complete.

195
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:19,280
And construction workers were ready to build the second inner liner inside the primary lining.

196
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:21,920
Except for one problem.

197
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:29,320
The south tunnel had drifted eight inches out of alignment as the huge tunneling machine dug through the ground.

198
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:39,220
The alignment of the tunnel is all designed for a certain radius of the rail and speed of the rail for the metro rail cars.

199
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:46,140
And if the rail comes too close to the tunnel wall, then the car would come too close to the wall during operation.

200
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:54,220
The contractor decided to fix the problem by moving the tunnel wall out eight inches away from the tracks.

201
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:58,740
Twenty essential four-foot support rings had to be partially dismantled.

202
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:02,780
workers removed the upper right corner of each ring

203
00:15:02,781 --> 00:15:06,820
then they dug out the dirt needed to widen the top of the passageway

204
00:15:06,821 --> 00:15:11,000
they replaced the upper corners with expanded steel segments

205
00:15:11,001 --> 00:15:14,760
creating an arch that extended beyond the bottom half of the rings

206
00:15:14,761 --> 00:15:18,540
the process was supposed to be repeated at the bottom of each ring

207
00:15:18,541 --> 00:15:21,380
so that they could reconnect the tops to the bottoms

208
00:15:21,381 --> 00:15:25,260
overlooking the fact that the soil was weak in this area

209
00:15:25,261 --> 00:15:29,220
and could not support itself the plan to replace the tops of all of the rings

210
00:15:29,221 --> 00:15:35,940
first turned out to be a colossal blunder. The disconnected rings could not hold up

211
00:15:35,941 --> 00:15:40,740
the 4,000 tons of soil that comprised the 80 feet of earth above them.

212
00:15:40,741 --> 00:15:46,580
The underlying problem was that by removing all the rings at the top first,

213
00:15:46,581 --> 00:15:51,940
they essentially took away the closed ring carrying capacity of the liner.

214
00:15:51,941 --> 00:15:56,900
There's nothing supporting the ring itself.

215
00:15:56,901 --> 00:16:01,900
After the accident, a new contractor was hired to finish the job.

216
00:16:01,901 --> 00:16:08,900
A decision was made to build a rectangular repair shaft around the site of the collapse.

217
00:16:08,901 --> 00:16:15,900
The walls of the shaft were formed by pounding steel casings into the ground and then filling them with concrete.

218
00:16:15,901 --> 00:16:21,900
The shaft stabilized the site, making it now possible to dig down from street level,

219
00:16:21,901 --> 00:16:25,040
excavating the lean mix and debris

220
00:16:25,041 --> 00:16:30,040
and clearing out the site without risking another cave in.

221
00:16:30,041 --> 00:16:32,960
Once all of the old debris was cleared from the inside of the shaft,

222
00:16:32,961 --> 00:16:38,420
the workers could rebuild the damaged tunnel.

223
00:16:38,421 --> 00:16:41,960
Instead of spending extra time and money to restore the concrete rings,

224
00:16:41,961 --> 00:16:47,040
they converted the shaft

225
00:16:47,041 --> 00:16:50,300
into a small reinforced rectangular station box,

226
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:52,360
similar to a subway station.

227
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:57,160
The new box section had tunnel openings on both ends

228
00:16:57,161 --> 00:16:58,660
to allow entry for the trains.

229
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:03,580
They just lay the rail across that box

230
00:17:03,581 --> 00:17:06,000
and traverse the box.

231
00:17:06,540 --> 00:17:08,780
Just like when the subway enters the station,

232
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:12,480
it comes out of a tube and into a box.

233
00:17:14,780 --> 00:17:16,920
Hollywood Boulevard's three-mile section

234
00:17:16,921 --> 00:17:18,960
of the 17-mile metro red line

235
00:17:18,961 --> 00:17:21,540
finally went into service in 1999.

236
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:26,160
Approximately 106,500 people

237
00:17:26,161 --> 00:17:29,300
ride the 17 miles of the Red Line every day.

238
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:33,900
But the Great Hollywood Boulevard tunnel collapse

239
00:17:33,901 --> 00:17:35,920
so traumatized some local politicians

240
00:17:35,921 --> 00:17:39,640
that plans for additional subway lines came to a halt.

241
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,080
Meanwhile, car pollution and traffic nightmares

242
00:17:44,081 --> 00:17:46,440
continue to plague the City of Angels.

243
00:17:46,441 --> 00:17:54,600
The two tunneling machines with backup gear that dug out the Metro Red Line were each approximately 100 yards in length.

244
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:58,440
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

245
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:11,120
Disaster lurks in the most unexpected places and often strikes when all is calm and peaceful.

246
00:18:11,121 --> 00:18:20,960
It was a quiet evening on December 17, 1976, at the Port of Los Angeles in San Pedro, California.

247
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:31,360
A Liberian oil tanker, the Sansanina, was moored here at birth number 46,

248
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:36,120
after unloading a cargo of 212,000 barrels of Indonesian crude.

249
00:18:36,121 --> 00:18:45,060
During the last remaining hours of daylight, her Italian crew refueled the ship and filled its empty cargo tanks with seawater.

250
00:18:46,040 --> 00:18:52,340
This is a routine practice called ballasting that would keep the tanker riding low in the water on her voyage back at sea.

251
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:57,500
What happened next would remain forever in the memory of those who were there.

252
00:18:57,501 --> 00:19:02,400
I was located at my station, at Fire Station 112 on Terminal Island.

253
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:08,440
About 7.35, somewhere along there in the evening, as I looked out that window,

254
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:12,460
a tremendous pressure wave hit us.

255
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,260
To tell you the truth, I thought somebody dropped a bomb.

256
00:19:16,140 --> 00:19:19,160
It sounded just like I looked out the window and a big mushroom went up in the air.

257
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:24,500
And I grabbed the fire phone, but it was dead. Everything had been cut off.

258
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:29,640
We call this a self-announcing alarm.

259
00:19:30,840 --> 00:19:32,240
Everybody ran for the boat.

260
00:19:32,780 --> 00:19:37,880
We weren't going to wait around to hear any kind of a signal come from the signal office.

261
00:19:38,540 --> 00:19:41,740
The force of the blast could be felt 40 miles away.

262
00:19:42,660 --> 00:19:45,820
In response, some 200 firefighters raced to the pier.

263
00:19:46,520 --> 00:19:52,420
The first thing we saw when we arrived on the scene was a large mass of fire involving the wharf

264
00:19:52,421 --> 00:19:56,220
to such an extent that you couldn't identify what actually was out there.

265
00:19:56,221 --> 00:20:01,080
It appeared to me that there was a large building there on the wharf that was on fire,

266
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:04,620
and yet I knew that there was no building there of that size.

267
00:20:05,420 --> 00:20:10,700
The building Dahlquist saw was a chunk of the 70,000-ton San Sanina.

268
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:16,320
Part of its midsection had blown out of the water and landed in the parking lot.

269
00:20:17,080 --> 00:20:20,000
The rest lay submerged at the bottom of the harbor.

270
00:20:20,660 --> 00:20:22,480
There was fire on top of the water.

271
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:24,020
There was fire on the wharf.

272
00:20:24,021 --> 00:20:28,920
It was fire on the land where we had this coke and other materials stored.

273
00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:34,280
We simply worked all of those areas with our monitors until we were able to extinguish the fire.

274
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:38,980
Remarkably, most of the 30 men aboard the Sansonina survived.

275
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:43,360
Thanks to the efforts of firemen like Walter Ball,

276
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:48,320
who maneuvered his fireboat through the flaming debris to get the Sansonina crew off the ship.

277
00:20:49,560 --> 00:20:52,940
About the first five, all of them they wanted to do was go, go, let's get out of here.

278
00:20:52,941 --> 00:20:55,300
they thought it was going to explode again.

279
00:20:55,301 --> 00:20:57,440
It was making all kinds of noises in the ship.

280
00:21:00,820 --> 00:21:03,940
At risk to his own life, Ball stayed put

281
00:21:03,941 --> 00:21:07,180
as the last of 18 men clambered down from the ship's stern.

282
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:10,660
Well, I had to get them forward or we're going to sink.

283
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:13,860
And so I got up in the flying bridge and away we went.

284
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:17,720
In the end, more than 50 people were injured

285
00:21:17,721 --> 00:21:19,420
and nine people were dead,

286
00:21:20,160 --> 00:21:22,500
including three men whose bodies were never found.

287
00:21:22,940 --> 00:21:28,600
The cause of the blast that claimed their lives and wrought so much destruction was a mystery.

288
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:36,460
Accidents are generally caused by a series of small items which fall into alignment,

289
00:21:37,040 --> 00:21:40,260
and it appeared that's what happened on the Sansonina that day.

290
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:46,580
The Coast Guard immediately launched an investigation into what caused the 18-year-old ship to explode.

291
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:51,480
They focused on what the Sansonina crew was doing prior to the blast.

292
00:21:52,460 --> 00:21:53,160
Ballasting.

293
00:21:53,600 --> 00:21:56,060
Well, in a tank, if you start to fill the water,

294
00:21:56,140 --> 00:21:57,600
of course the water goes to the bottom of the tank,

295
00:21:57,940 --> 00:22:00,720
and what it does is it forces the fumes out the top of the tank.

296
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:05,480
Most hydrocarbon fumes, like gasoline fumes, are heavier than air.

297
00:22:05,481 --> 00:22:07,300
So what that means is when they get up,

298
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:10,300
they don't readily dissipate up into the air.

299
00:22:10,540 --> 00:22:14,600
It sits there until it's driven off by the wind or something of that nature.

300
00:22:15,480 --> 00:22:18,780
In most circumstances, the fumes would have been carried off by the wind.

301
00:22:18,781 --> 00:22:22,680
But on the day of the explosion, there was no wind,

302
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:25,580
and the highly flammable vapor cloud was trapped

303
00:22:25,581 --> 00:22:28,640
between the midship deckhouse and the aft castle.

304
00:22:29,700 --> 00:22:33,060
Well, if you have a huge volume of hydrocarbon mixed with air,

305
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:38,840
and you ignite this, what you have created in reality

306
00:22:38,841 --> 00:22:41,280
is what we call now a fuel-air bomb.

307
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:46,260
And the effect, of course, can be devastating.

308
00:22:46,261 --> 00:22:51,300
What caused the vapor cloud to ignite will perhaps never be known.

309
00:22:52,360 --> 00:22:54,580
There were too many potential sources.

310
00:22:55,340 --> 00:23:00,360
Once you have hydrocarbon gas in the right ratio of fuel and air to be ignited,

311
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:04,020
it will find an ignition source sooner or later.

312
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:07,180
Trust me, on an industrial system, there'll be an electric spark,

313
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:09,140
there'll be something being hot,

314
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:12,380
and it doesn't take much energy to kick this off.

315
00:23:12,381 --> 00:23:16,520
It isn't just what collects on deck.

316
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:19,040
That's what causes and finds a source of ignition.

317
00:23:19,620 --> 00:23:24,220
There were conduction paths of air-fuel mixtures down into the tanks.

318
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:29,380
The San Sanina had safety and ventilation systems in place

319
00:23:29,381 --> 00:23:31,880
to prevent a fire from ever reaching its cargo tanks.

320
00:23:32,380 --> 00:23:37,880
The chief mate was supposed to have some flame screens in place

321
00:23:37,881 --> 00:23:42,520
over openings which were venting out onto the deck.

322
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:46,920
The flame screens are intended to prevent any flame

323
00:23:46,921 --> 00:23:49,760
from going into the cargo tanks on the ship.

324
00:23:50,500 --> 00:23:52,500
That was an industry practice at the time

325
00:23:52,501 --> 00:23:54,540
and appears they were not in place.

326
00:23:55,200 --> 00:23:57,560
It also appears from the Coast Guard reports

327
00:23:57,561 --> 00:24:00,040
that some of the gas lines which were intended

328
00:24:00,041 --> 00:24:04,980
to carry gas vapors away to safer areas may have been rusted.

329
00:24:04,981 --> 00:24:08,920
had the vents not been so rusted.

330
00:24:09,920 --> 00:24:11,420
They might have had a flash fire on deck,

331
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:13,820
but they may not have had the big explosion of the ship.

332
00:24:14,340 --> 00:24:18,300
But all these things came together that day wrong,

333
00:24:19,100 --> 00:24:20,200
so a number of people died.

334
00:24:21,980 --> 00:24:25,680
In 1977, almost a year after the accident,

335
00:24:26,180 --> 00:24:29,860
the Coast Guard completed their investigation and issued a report.

336
00:24:30,840 --> 00:24:34,380
Their analysis of what happened had far-reaching effects in the maritime industry.

337
00:24:34,980 --> 00:24:41,540
Today, there are significant safety features on tankers to prevent what happened to the

338
00:24:41,541 --> 00:24:44,300
Sansanina.

339
00:24:44,301 --> 00:24:47,020
Foremost are inert gas systems.

340
00:24:47,021 --> 00:24:54,220
The theory is that by having no oxygen inside the cargo tank, it will not support combustion.

341
00:24:54,221 --> 00:25:01,060
If there is insufficient oxygen, there is no possibility of an explosion or fire.

342
00:25:01,061 --> 00:25:07,060
A typical inert gas system involves taking gases from the ship's boilers and feeding them into the cargo tanks.

343
00:25:07,860 --> 00:25:09,480
This drives out all the oxygen.

344
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:16,440
Without oxygen, vapors left in the tank are inert and therefore not combustible.

345
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:24,960
Even though inert gas systems were in use in the 1970s, there were no requirements that the Sansonina have one.

346
00:25:26,340 --> 00:25:29,480
Today, no supertanker operates without one.

347
00:25:31,060 --> 00:25:33,740
In addition, venting systems are more sophisticated,

348
00:25:34,540 --> 00:25:37,780
so that hydrocarbon vapors are expelled high above the ship

349
00:25:37,781 --> 00:25:39,800
and unlikely to pool on deck.

350
00:25:42,300 --> 00:25:43,600
Ballasting is still routine,

351
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:46,840
but modern tankers no longer mix oil and water.

352
00:25:47,980 --> 00:25:50,020
Ballast tanks are separate from cargo tanks.

353
00:25:50,620 --> 00:25:52,660
If you look at the major disasters in history,

354
00:25:52,661 --> 00:25:56,080
you see in retrospect a sequence of mistakes,

355
00:25:56,081 --> 00:26:01,220
each one of which at the time appeared to be innocent and inconsequential

356
00:26:01,221 --> 00:26:07,660
which all combined in a very unfortunate way at the time of the disaster to create a disaster.

357
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:13,320
It took an 18-year-old ship called the Sansonina and a horrific accident

358
00:26:13,321 --> 00:26:19,760
to expose dangers that stalked oil tankers at sea and at rest in quiet harbors.

359
00:26:19,761 --> 00:26:27,420
The 1989 Exxon Valdez spill of 37,000 metric tons of oil is not the largest on record.

360
00:26:27,940 --> 00:26:32,880
The Atlantic Empress spilled 287,000 metric tons of oil in 1979.

361
00:26:33,880 --> 00:26:36,400
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

362
00:26:36,401 --> 00:26:44,700
nearly a hundred years ago

363
00:26:44,701 --> 00:26:47,700
this quiet Boston neighborhood called the North End

364
00:26:47,701 --> 00:26:51,180
was the scene of one of the most bizarre engineering disasters

365
00:26:51,181 --> 00:26:52,580
in U.S. history

366
00:26:52,581 --> 00:26:55,960
a colossal storage tank stood here

367
00:26:55,961 --> 00:26:58,680
in what was once a busy commercial and residential area

368
00:26:58,681 --> 00:27:02,320
it was five stories tall

369
00:27:02,321 --> 00:27:04,840
242 feet in circumference

370
00:27:04,841 --> 00:27:06,440
and 90 feet wide.

371
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:11,240
The tank contained 2.3 million gallons of molasses.

372
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:16,040
Without warning, on January 15, 1919,

373
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:18,420
the storage tank ruptured,

374
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,740
spewing a thick, dark wave of destruction.

375
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:29,620
26 million pounds of molasses spilled out at 35 miles per hour.

376
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:33,300
It wasn't just the molasses that wreaked havoc.

377
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:37,440
Rivets and fragments from the tank shot out like shrapnel.

378
00:27:38,380 --> 00:27:42,020
One piece crashed into an overhead train trestle, causing it to buckle.

379
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:49,320
The flood claimed 21 lives, injured 150, and caused more than a million dollars in damage.

380
00:27:50,060 --> 00:27:52,280
It took months to clean up the sticky mess.

381
00:27:53,240 --> 00:27:59,460
The wave smashed into a nearby firehouse and caused the second floor to pancake down onto the first

382
00:27:59,461 --> 00:28:02,960
and trapped the firefighters in an 18-inch crawl space.

383
00:28:03,300 --> 00:28:06,420
It ended up killing one of the firefighters, George Leahy,

384
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:11,680
who stays alive for between two and three hours by keeping his head above the molasses

385
00:28:11,681 --> 00:28:15,220
until he no longer can do so and he succumbs and he drowns.

386
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:19,320
At the time, no one was sure what caused the eruption.

387
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:29,040
Four years earlier, in 1915, a company called United States Industrial Alcohol, or USIA, had built the tank.

388
00:28:29,740 --> 00:28:32,640
Molasses was a key ingredient in the company's namesake product.

389
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:38,940
Molasses is this black, sticky stuff that's left over when you process sugar cane.

390
00:28:39,460 --> 00:28:43,680
You make alcohol from molasses, you dilute it with water, you add yeast,

391
00:28:44,160 --> 00:28:46,120
you run it through a still, and you've got rum.

392
00:28:46,720 --> 00:28:51,360
Industrial alcohol is the same as rum, but it's distilled to a very high proof

393
00:28:51,361 --> 00:28:54,740
and as light and flavorless as they can make it.

394
00:28:55,720 --> 00:29:00,340
Industrial alcohol is a solvent or a liquid compound that dissolves other materials.

395
00:29:01,340 --> 00:29:05,100
It's also a key ingredient in the manufacture of munitions.

396
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:13,360
World War I was heating up in Europe, and demand for the product was on the rise.

397
00:29:14,060 --> 00:29:18,740
Industrial alcohol would have been used to make explosives for firing shells

398
00:29:18,741 --> 00:29:20,780
and for having them blow up on the other end.

399
00:29:21,080 --> 00:29:23,320
There were all kinds of military uses for it.

400
00:29:24,720 --> 00:29:29,240
Company treasurer Arthur P. Gell was put in charge of a new Boston terminal

401
00:29:29,241 --> 00:29:31,900
where molasses could be offloaded by steamers

402
00:29:31,901 --> 00:29:35,420
and transported by rail to a company plant in East Cambridge.

403
00:29:37,300 --> 00:29:39,220
It was up to him to build a tank

404
00:29:39,221 --> 00:29:41,980
that could store more than 2 million gallons of molasses.

405
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:46,200
He had no engineering experience,

406
00:29:46,380 --> 00:29:48,600
couldn't read plans, couldn't read blueprints,

407
00:29:49,060 --> 00:29:50,900
had no technical experience whatsoever.

408
00:29:51,060 --> 00:29:52,640
He was basically a finance person,

409
00:29:53,080 --> 00:29:56,160
yet he was charged with the construction of this tank.

410
00:29:56,161 --> 00:29:59,280
He had to get the tank built, and he had to get the tank built fast.

411
00:30:00,740 --> 00:30:05,360
Jell hired the Hammond Ironworks Company of Boston to manufacture and assemble the tank.

412
00:30:07,260 --> 00:30:10,140
They would build the tank using lap joint construction,

413
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,800
a method in which one steel band overlapped another.

414
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:20,480
A horizontal band of 18 plates formed the tank's cylindrical shape.

415
00:30:21,940 --> 00:30:25,560
The tank's height would be achieved by overlapping seven of these rows.

416
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:35,400
Their specifications said they were going to furnish certain thickness steel bands for the tank,

417
00:30:35,640 --> 00:30:40,200
larger at the bottom and then continually smaller to the top.

418
00:30:40,980 --> 00:30:44,600
The specs were not met. It was a rush job.

419
00:30:45,560 --> 00:30:49,660
U.S. industrial alcohol had to have the tank ready by the end of 1915.

420
00:30:50,660 --> 00:30:55,680
A delivery of 700,000 gallons of molasses was on its way from Cuba to Boston.

421
00:30:56,160 --> 00:31:02,640
There's no documentation that they tested the steel or that the rivets were properly installed or any of that stuff.

422
00:31:02,740 --> 00:31:11,320
They did check the tank for leakage by running in six or eight inches of water,

423
00:31:11,700 --> 00:31:14,640
but that was hardly a test of the tank's strength.

424
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:21,420
On December 29th, two days before the molasses steamer arrived, the tank was finished.

425
00:31:22,140 --> 00:31:24,220
But from the start, there were signs of trouble.

426
00:31:24,221 --> 00:31:31,280
It was leaking at the seams near the lower portion of the tank, which would have the extreme pressures on it.

427
00:31:32,600 --> 00:31:38,440
Over the next three years, USIA did nothing to seriously address the problem other than to caulk the leaks.

428
00:31:39,200 --> 00:31:43,440
By the fall of 1918, there were more pressing matters that the company faced.

429
00:31:44,820 --> 00:31:47,760
World War I was ending, and so would their profits.

430
00:31:48,720 --> 00:31:55,520
To tide it over, USIA decided to retool its manufacturing plants to produce alcohol for the liquor industries.

431
00:31:57,480 --> 00:31:59,618
It would have been very easy for U.S.

432
00:31:59,619 --> 00:32:03,720
industrial alcohol to switch production from industrial alcohol to rum.

433
00:32:04,460 --> 00:32:07,680
They just would have had to design a bottle and put a little extra water in it.

434
00:32:08,780 --> 00:32:11,660
But the company only had a small window of opportunity.

435
00:32:11,661 --> 00:32:16,720
Prohibition was likely to go into effect in 1920,

436
00:32:17,100 --> 00:32:20,500
so USIA had less than a year to get its product out the door.

437
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:24,660
Some states had already had prohibition for a good number of years,

438
00:32:24,661 --> 00:32:28,200
but in general it took a while to get the enforcement up and running.

439
00:32:29,560 --> 00:32:34,980
In November 1918, USIA ordered one last huge shipment of molasses from Cuba.

440
00:32:36,140 --> 00:32:39,140
It arrived January 12, 1919.

441
00:32:39,141 --> 00:32:46,140
That day, more than two million gallons of molasses were transferred from ship to tank.

442
00:32:46,141 --> 00:32:53,140
Three days later, the pressure inside the tank proved to be too much.

443
00:32:57,140 --> 00:33:04,140
The molasses flood sparked one of the first class action lawsuits in the country.

444
00:33:04,141 --> 00:33:08,620
But USIA argued that sinister forces were responsible for the disaster.

445
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:16,600
The cornerstone of U.S. industrial alcohol's defense in the case

446
00:33:16,601 --> 00:33:20,840
was that an anarchist had planted a bomb on the molasses tank site

447
00:33:20,841 --> 00:33:23,180
and caused the tank to explode.

448
00:33:24,520 --> 00:33:26,880
But that theory was quickly disproved.

449
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:29,940
Most broken windows were below the floodline.

450
00:33:29,941 --> 00:33:33,340
In other words, there was no concussive force.

451
00:33:34,140 --> 00:33:36,660
However, there was much evidence of shoddy construction.

452
00:33:37,620 --> 00:33:39,980
The tank was built in a substandard way.

453
00:33:40,360 --> 00:33:43,280
It was overseen by a person who didn't really know what he was doing.

454
00:33:43,640 --> 00:33:50,320
The steel plates that were used to construct the tank were much thinner than originally called for by the plants.

455
00:33:50,680 --> 00:33:56,460
Not enough rivets were used in the construction of the tank, and the tank was not tested properly.

456
00:33:56,600 --> 00:33:58,560
In fact, it really wasn't tested at all.

457
00:33:58,561 --> 00:34:00,817
A judge ruled against U.S.

458
00:34:00,818 --> 00:34:07,240
industrial alcohol, and the company agreed to pay $628,000 to the victims.

459
00:34:10,940 --> 00:34:14,880
The molasses flood is more than a strange footnote in Boston history.

460
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:22,440
The event had an enormous impact on the construction industry, paving the way for tougher regulations.

461
00:34:22,441 --> 00:34:29,640
Just about all of the safety standards that we are used to today, the fact that engineers

462
00:34:29,641 --> 00:34:34,080
need to stamp their plans and architects need to sign their work emanated from the great

463
00:34:34,081 --> 00:34:35,100
Boston Molasses Foot.

464
00:34:35,700 --> 00:34:40,240
If that tank were to be built again, they not only would have stamped and sealed drawings

465
00:34:40,241 --> 00:34:45,840
and calculations, they'd also have a follow-up construction observation program that show

466
00:34:45,841 --> 00:34:50,520
that they were furnishing what they said they were and that they were installing the rivets

467
00:34:50,521 --> 00:34:51,221
properly.

468
00:34:52,440 --> 00:34:56,900
Testing today is also a crucial and standard part of the engineering process.

469
00:34:57,860 --> 00:35:03,640
And we owe it all to a strange and deadly disaster involving molasses.

470
00:35:06,700 --> 00:35:09,240
Molasses was once America's top sweetener.

471
00:35:09,860 --> 00:35:14,680
It was only after World War I, when sugar prices dropped, that sugar moved to number one.

472
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:19,100
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

473
00:35:22,440 --> 00:35:33,800
In 1977, the Indiana Department of Transportation broke ground on State Road 912, a 15-mile

474
00:35:33,801 --> 00:35:39,540
stretch of highway built to attach the Chicago Skyway to Interstate 80, one of America's

475
00:35:39,541 --> 00:35:40,660
coast-to-coast highways.

476
00:35:41,600 --> 00:35:45,220
Indiana DOT built this road as a six-lane highway.

477
00:35:46,380 --> 00:35:52,280
It serves as the front door to a major industrial area for steel mills, oil refineries, heavy

478
00:35:52,281 --> 00:35:53,787
industry of all kinds.

479
00:35:53,788 --> 00:35:58,580
At the time, this project was the largest construction project Indiana

480
00:35:58,581 --> 00:35:59,847
DOT had ever built.

481
00:35:59,848 --> 00:36:04,380
The plans for the new highway included the construction of the Kline

482
00:36:04,381 --> 00:36:12,800
Avenue Bridge, a 6,260-foot-long, 130-foot-high concrete box girder bridge, to cross over

483
00:36:12,801 --> 00:36:15,814
one of Lake Michigan's major shipping canals.

484
00:36:15,815 --> 00:36:18,560
The addition of the new bridge would ease

485
00:36:18,561 --> 00:36:22,000
traffic congestion on the local lift bridge.

486
00:36:22,001 --> 00:36:26,240
A lift bridge was the only way across the canal right in the heart of this industrial

487
00:36:26,241 --> 00:36:29,240
area.

488
00:36:29,241 --> 00:36:37,200
When a ship came in and the lift bridge lifted, all traffic was stopped until the ship cleared.

489
00:36:37,201 --> 00:36:42,440
One of the purposes of this new bridge was to go over the top of this canal to provide

490
00:36:42,441 --> 00:36:45,880
access from clearance for the boats underneath and not to stop traffic.

491
00:36:48,560 --> 00:36:50,800
Construction of the bridge began in 1980.

492
00:36:52,060 --> 00:36:58,560
By 1982, one-third of the bridge's sections were in place, and production was on schedule.

493
00:36:59,820 --> 00:37:10,940
But on April 15, 1982, as construction workers poured concrete into the roadbed for a 180-foot span of the bridge's on-ramp, disaster struck.

494
00:37:12,940 --> 00:37:15,780
The ramp buckled and suddenly collapsed.

495
00:37:18,560 --> 00:37:32,560
A few minutes later, another 180-foot section gave way and crumbled to the ground, flipping over a third section, which dangled precariously in the air by frayed

496
00:37:32,628 --> 00:37:33,328
cables.

497
00:37:33,960 --> 00:37:40,220
It was just as if a bomb had landed and just everything was exploded and everything was utter chaos.

498
00:37:40,440 --> 00:37:42,680
And all you could hear was people screaming.

499
00:37:42,681 --> 00:37:51,520
I was in a state of shock when I arrived at the job and saw the damage and the mass confusion

500
00:37:51,521 --> 00:37:55,920
and the debris of concrete and steel twisted on the ground.

501
00:37:56,580 --> 00:38:02,940
There were people covered with fresh concrete because they were pouring hundreds of cubic yards of concrete

502
00:38:02,941 --> 00:38:09,600
70 feet in the air and it fell along with the bridge and trapped people in the concrete during the collapse.

503
00:38:12,680 --> 00:38:16,340
Twelve people were killed, and 18 were seriously injured.

504
00:38:17,340 --> 00:38:20,400
It was the worst construction disaster in Indiana's history.

505
00:38:27,660 --> 00:38:31,860
As structural engineers from the National Institute of Standards and Technology

506
00:38:31,861 --> 00:38:36,340
searched for clues, they began to trace the origins of the collapse

507
00:38:36,341 --> 00:38:39,300
all the way to the selection of the construction method.

508
00:38:39,301 --> 00:38:47,540
This project was somewhat unique in that the contractor was allowed to select what kind of bridge he built.

509
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:55,860
The contractor, Superior Construction, chose to employ the cast-in-place post-tensioned box girder technique.

510
00:38:56,600 --> 00:39:01,480
It's cast at the final position rather than pre-cast,

511
00:39:01,481 --> 00:39:09,160
which is a system in which you cast the members in a factory and then ship them to the site and put them in place.

512
00:39:09,300 --> 00:39:16,620
It's a very strong, very light form of construction that has been pioneered, I would say, over the last 30 years.

513
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:21,460
It started in this country with the Walnut Lane Bridge in Philadelphia in 1951.

514
00:39:22,220 --> 00:39:26,400
And since that time, that type of construction has really taken off.

515
00:39:27,100 --> 00:39:35,680
Since cast-in-place bridges are hollow in the middle, they require a minimum amount of concrete to get a maximum amount of strength.

516
00:39:35,681 --> 00:39:39,680
That makes them extremely cost effective.

517
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:43,440
During construction of the Klein Avenue Bridge,

518
00:39:44,300 --> 00:39:48,340
permanent pillars and temporary towers supported elevated roadbed forms,

519
00:39:48,840 --> 00:39:51,000
while workers poured the concrete into place.

520
00:39:51,880 --> 00:39:54,460
The towers were made of steel.

521
00:39:54,461 --> 00:39:56,980
They come in prefabricated units,

522
00:39:57,240 --> 00:40:00,260
and the contractor simply puts these units together,

523
00:40:00,440 --> 00:40:04,140
sort of a Lego fashion to build different heights.

524
00:40:05,680 --> 00:40:10,260
These steel towers sat on concrete pads that were built to stabilize the legs.

525
00:40:10,800 --> 00:40:14,820
The temporary support system is a vital part of the structure.

526
00:40:14,821 --> 00:40:22,160
Until the superstructure is completed, the temporary support system provides the basic support of the ramp,

527
00:40:22,340 --> 00:40:28,720
and it was what was holding the ramp up at the time of the collapse, so it had to be the source of the collapse.

528
00:40:29,560 --> 00:40:33,420
There was no requirement for the strength of the concrete in the pads,

529
00:40:33,580 --> 00:40:36,580
which is a real surprise to us.

530
00:40:36,581 --> 00:40:41,080
The pads were sort of cast as an afterthought.

531
00:40:41,580 --> 00:40:45,580
That is, if there was any concrete left over from casting the structure above,

532
00:40:46,120 --> 00:40:52,060
that concrete was used in the pads of the succeeding parts of the structure.

533
00:40:53,280 --> 00:40:56,760
When the structural engineers finally removed all of the debris

534
00:40:56,761 --> 00:40:59,300
that had buried the critical concrete support pads.

535
00:40:59,820 --> 00:41:02,720
They discovered the pads had cracks.

536
00:41:03,260 --> 00:41:05,360
We did not believe that those cracks

537
00:41:05,361 --> 00:41:08,300
were a result of impact during collapse

538
00:41:08,301 --> 00:41:12,420
because the debris resting on top of the slabs

539
00:41:12,421 --> 00:41:15,220
had no indication of a direct impact.

540
00:41:15,660 --> 00:41:18,560
So we concluded from that that the cracks

541
00:41:18,561 --> 00:41:21,140
that we observed in those critical pads

542
00:41:21,141 --> 00:41:25,340
were a result of their being overloaded during construction.

543
00:41:26,760 --> 00:41:32,400
To prove the theory, the engineers took core samples of the concrete pads to a lab to be tested.

544
00:41:33,160 --> 00:41:40,900
As a designer, you'd like to have the strength of a structural element be roughly two times the load that will be on it.

545
00:41:41,900 --> 00:41:49,820
We put the core in a giant press and we apply a compression load on it until it can't support that load anymore.

546
00:41:49,821 --> 00:41:55,940
From that compressive strength, we can estimate what the flexural strength of the concrete is,

547
00:41:56,040 --> 00:42:00,820
which is the way the slabs supporting the shoring towers would fail.

548
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:08,140
And we use that estimate to calculate the capacity of the entire slab to resist the vertical load.

549
00:42:09,580 --> 00:42:14,060
Tests verified that the concrete pads were only half as strong as they should have been.

550
00:42:14,960 --> 00:42:17,680
They could not support the weight of the shoring towers.

551
00:42:19,820 --> 00:42:25,780
The weakness of a single pad triggered a chain reaction that caused the whole system to collapse.

552
00:42:26,440 --> 00:42:33,000
We believe that one of the concrete pads cracked, and that led to cracking of another pad.

553
00:42:33,600 --> 00:42:40,020
Both pads were now cracked, and this caused the tops of the shoring towers to shift in one direction.

554
00:42:40,021 --> 00:42:46,720
This puts a tremendous bending stress on the U-heads, which we know were poorly constructed.

555
00:42:46,721 --> 00:42:54,600
That caused the U-heads to break, and as a result, the crossbeam that was supported by those U-heads falls to the ground.

556
00:42:55,080 --> 00:43:00,360
Now we have all of the weight of the structure on one side of the shoring towers,

557
00:43:00,680 --> 00:43:05,300
and what happened is the shoring towers buckled and the ramp collapsed.

558
00:43:07,420 --> 00:43:11,160
Construction of the Klein Avenue Bridge resumed later in 1982.

559
00:43:11,161 --> 00:43:17,440
The National Institute of Standards and Technology and an independent consulting firm

560
00:43:17,441 --> 00:43:21,980
offered recommendations for the construction of the new temporary support system.

561
00:43:23,440 --> 00:43:29,220
To ensure they met safety criteria, Superior Construction created a separate design plan

562
00:43:29,221 --> 00:43:31,020
for the new temporary support structure.

563
00:43:32,200 --> 00:43:37,540
Instead of using concrete pads, larger wooden timber footings were constructed to support

564
00:43:37,541 --> 00:43:38,360
the tower legs.

565
00:43:38,361 --> 00:43:43,660
These footings were bolted together to form a long, solid foundation.

566
00:43:44,400 --> 00:43:52,660
I think the key lesson from this investigation is the design of a temporary support system in construction.

567
00:43:53,280 --> 00:44:01,180
It needs to have the same detail of attention and engineering calculation as the final structure that's being built.

568
00:44:01,940 --> 00:44:06,080
The Klein Avenue bridge was finally completed in late 1983.

569
00:44:08,360 --> 00:44:11,660
Over 20,000 vehicles cross this bridge every day.

570
00:44:13,700 --> 00:44:18,260
To commemorate those who died in the state's worst ever construction disaster,

571
00:44:19,200 --> 00:44:26,780
the Indiana Department of Transportation renamed the 15-mile stretch of State Road 912 in their honor.

